The current board has pieces concentrated in columns 2 and 3, with x occupying (row5,col3), (row3,col3) and (row1,col3) while o occupies (row5,col2), (row4,col3) and (row2,col3). No player can achieve four-in-a-row on this turn (or even three-in-a-row), because the existing pieces of each color are insufficiently aligned and the only candidate extension squares lie too high to be reached in a single drop.

The empty square at (row4,col2) is immediately accessible and strategically decisive. Placing an x there simultaneously:
- unites two previously disconnected x-pieces into a single connected group of three via the two diagonals (row5,col3)–(row4,col2) and (row4,col2)–(row3,col3);
- denies o the same square, which would otherwise have let o form a compact connected trio of its own (vertical + horizontal).

All other legal drops are inferior on the same metric:
- column 4 yields only a single new adjacency and leaves the valuable bridge at (row4,col2) for o;
- column 3 merely adds a non-central top-row piece;
- wings (columns 0/1/5/6) create no connections at all.

By taking the bridge now, x obtains the connection on its own tempo. o may answer by connecting on the opposite side (column 4), after which x still retains the next free move to begin constructing a concrete threat. Consequently the unique optimal drop is column 2.

Final Answer: 2

